ion-specific engineering guidance\n\nDescribe the internal components involved, their responsibilities, the API interactions, reconciliation behavior, and the order in which operations occur. Explain which component owns each decision and how the system behaves when a dependency becomes unavailable.\n\nWhen documenting this subject, record the resource name, namespace, labels, selectors, relevant annotations, configuration values, dependencies, and expected status. Explain how the feature behaves during normal operation and what changes when a dependency becomes unhealthy. Include the operational effect on application availability, deployment speed, recovery time, and resource consumption.\n\n### 6. Verification and troubleshooting\n\nFor EKS-specific verification, confirm the AWS account and Region, cluster identity, endpoint access, and configured authentication mechanism. Use the same IAM principal or workload identity that the actual application or operator uses. A successful AWS CLI command does not by itself prove that Kubernetes RBAC permits the requested operation.\n\nThe Kubernetes control plane coordinates the cluster through the API server, persistent cluster state, scheduler, and controller managers. The API server validates and exposes API operations. etcd stores cluster state. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to appropriate nodes. Controllers continuously reconcile resources such as Deployments, ReplicaSets, Nodes, and endpoints. Understanding these responsibilities helps isolate API failures, scheduling delays, reconciliation problems, and cluster-wide outages.\n\nStart troubleshooting by capturing the current state before changing anything. Inspect resource status, events, controller logs, Pod descriptions, and application logs. Check whether the issue affects one Pod, one node, one namespace, or the entire cluster. Compare a failing resource with a healthy resource where possible. Identify the responsible component before selecting a correctiv