        "id": "stage-10-topic-7-section-11",
        "title": "Scheduler and Kubelet — Persistent Storage",
        "content": "## Scheduler and Kubelet — Persistent Storage\n\n### 1. Detailed technical explanation\n\nExplain the storage request, provisioning mechanism, volume attachment, mount lifecycle, access mode, and relationship between the Pod and persistent data. Include scheduling and availability-zone constraints, filesystem permissions, and safe data-retention considerations.\n\nThe Kubernetes control plane coordinates the cluster through the API server, persistent cluster state, scheduler, and controller managers. The API server validates and exposes API operations. etcd stores cluster state. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to appropriate nodes. Controllers continuously reconcile resources such as Deployments, ReplicaSets, Nodes, and endpoints. Understanding these responsibilities helps isolate API failures, scheduling delays, reconciliation problems, and cluster-wide outages.\n\n### 2. Purpose and engineering relevance\n\nThe purpose of this subject is to help engineers understand how 'Scheduler and Kubelet — Persistent Storage' affects the behavior of 'Scheduler and Kubelet' in a Kubernetes or Amazon EKS environment. It is important to understand the desired state, the live state, and the Kubernetes component responsible for reconciling differences between them. Kubernetes resources are declarative: engineers describe the intended configuration, submit it through the API, and controllers work continuously to make the cluster match that configuration.\n\nA DevOps engineer should understand which resource owns the configuration, which controller processes it, which dependent resources are created, and how the result becomes visible to the application. This knowledge is necessary when designing deployments, reviewing manifests, troubleshooting incidents, and planning production changes.\n\n### 3. Internal workflow and component interaction\n\nThe Kubernetes control plane coordinates the cluster through the API server, persistent cluster state, scheduler,