        "title": "Rolling Updates and Rollbacks — Cluster and Control Plane Operations",
        "content": "## Rolling Updates and Rollbacks — Cluster and Control Plane Operations\n\n### 1. Detailed technical explanation\n\nExplain what state or workload data must be protected, which recovery mechanism applies, what dependencies must be restored, and how recovery is validated. Distinguish restoring Kubernetes resource definitions from restoring persistent application data.\n\nA Deployment declares the desired state for a stateless application and manages ReplicaSets that maintain the requested number of Pods. When the Pod template changes, Kubernetes creates a new ReplicaSet and performs the configured rollout strategy. Readiness checks determine when new Pods can receive traffic. Rollout status, revision history, availability limits, and rollback procedures are essential for safe application releases.\n\n### 2. Purpose and engineering relevance\n\nThe purpose of this subject is to help engineers understand how 'Rolling Updates and Rollbacks — Cluster and Control Plane Operations' affects the behavior of 'Rolling Updates and Rollbacks' in a Kubernetes or Amazon EKS environment. It is important to understand the desired state, the live state, and the Kubernetes component responsible for reconciling differences between them. Kubernetes resources are declarative: engineers describe the intended configuration, submit it through the API, and controllers work continuously to make the cluster match that configuration.\n\nA DevOps engineer should understand which resource owns the configuration, which controller processes it, which dependent resources are created, and how the result becomes visible to the application. This knowledge is necessary when designing deployments, reviewing manifests, troubleshooting incidents, and planning production changes.\n\n### 3. Internal workflow and component interaction\n\nA Deployment declares the desired state for a stateless application and manages ReplicaSets that maintain the requested number of Pods. When the Pod templ