        "id": "stage-10-topic-22-section-12",
        "title": "Production Hands-on Lab — Autoscaling and Capacity",
        "content": "## Production Hands-on Lab — Autoscaling and Capacity\n\n### 1. Detailed technical explanation\n\nExplain node registration, resource availability, kubelet responsibilities, container runtime behavior, scheduling eligibility, and node health. For EKS, connect the Kubernetes node lifecycle to the selected compute model and AWS infrastructure dependencies.\n\nProduction Kubernetes operations require repeatable deployment procedures, capacity planning, access controls, health checks, observability, backup and recovery planning, and controlled change management. Workloads should define resource requests and limits, appropriate probes, replica requirements, disruption budgets, and secure configuration. Deployment validation should confirm both Kubernetes resource health and actual application behavior.\n\n### 2. Purpose and engineering relevance\n\nThe purpose of this subject is to help engineers understand how 'Production Hands-on Lab — Autoscaling and Capacity' affects the behavior of 'Production Hands-on Lab' in a Kubernetes or Amazon EKS environment. It is important to understand the desired state, the live state, and the Kubernetes component responsible for reconciling differences between them. Kubernetes resources are declarative: engineers describe the intended configuration, submit it through the API, and controllers work continuously to make the cluster match that configuration.\n\nA DevOps engineer should understand which resource owns the configuration, which controller processes it, which dependent resources are created, and how the result becomes visible to the application. This knowledge is necessary when designing deployments, reviewing manifests, troubleshooting incidents, and planning production changes.\n\n### 3. Internal workflow and component interaction\n\nProduction Kubernetes operations require repeatable deployment procedures, capacity planning, access controls, health checks, observability, backup and recovery planning, and controlled change management. Workloads should define resource requests and limits, appropriate probes, replica requirements, disruption budgets, and secure configuration. Deployment validation should confirm both Kubernetes resource health and actual application behavior.\n\nThe normal engineering workflow is to inspect the existing namespace and resources, review the relevant manifest or configuration, validate the intended changes, apply them through an approved deployment process, and inspect the resulting resource conditions. The API server is the entry point for Kubernetes API operations. Controllers reconcile desired state, the scheduler makes placement decisions for unscheduled Pods, and kubelet manages assigned workloads on worker nodes.\n\nThe exact components involved depend on the resource being configured. A Service relies on selectors and endpoints; a Deployment relies on ReplicaSets and Pods; a PersistentVolumeClaim relies on storage provisioning and binding; and an Ingress relies on an implementing controller. Understanding these dependencies prevents engineers from treating a symptom as the root cause.\n\n### 4. Practical commands and implementation\n\nThe following commands provide a starting point for inspecting the relevant Kubernetes or EKS environment. Replace placeholder values with the real namespace, resource name, cluster name, and AWS Region. Read command output before making changes.\n\nkubectl get deployments,pods,services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nkubectl rollout status deployment/DEPLOYMENT_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n\nUse the commands as diagnostic examples. For a configuration change, review the manifest, validate its syntax and resource fields, inspect the proposed change, and apply it through the repository's normal CI/CD process. Avoid applying an unreviewed manifest directly to a production cluster.\n\n### 5. Section-specific engineering guidance\n\nExplain node registration, resource availability, kubelet responsibilities, container runtime behavior, scheduling eligibility, and node health. For EKS, connect the Kubernetes node lifecycle to the selected compute model and AWS infrastructure dependencies.\n\nWhen documenting this subject, record the resource name, namespace, labels, selectors, relevant annotations, configuration values, dependencies, and expected status. Explain how the feature behaves during normal operation and what changes when a dependency becomes unhealthy. Include the operational effect on application availability, deployment speed, recovery time, and resource consumption.\n\n### 6. Verification and troubleshooting\n\nFor verification, inspect the live Kubernetes resource, its status conditions, related Pods, controller state, and recent events. Compare the observed state with the declared desired state. Confirm application behavior using readiness, logs, service connectivity, and relevant metrics rather than relying only on an API request returning successfully.\n\nProduction Kubernetes operations require repeatable deployment procedures, capacity planning, access controls, health checks, observability, backup and recovery planning, and controlled change management. Workloads should define resource requests and limits, appropriate probes, replica requirements, disruption budgets, and secure configuration. Deployment validation should confirm both Kubernetes resource health and actual application behavior.\n\nStart troubleshooting by capturing the current state before changing anything. Inspect resource status, events, controller logs, Pod descriptions, and application logs. Check whether the issue affects one Pod, one node, one namespace, or the entire cluster. Compare a failing resource with a healthy resource where possible. Identify the responsible component before selecting a corrective action.\n\nAfter remediation, verify that the resource reaches its expected status, dependent resources become healthy, application traffic succeeds, and no new warning events appear. Record the root cause and the corrective action so the same failure can be detected earlier in future deployments.\n\n### 7. CI/CD and DevOps integration\n\nIn a production CI/CD workflow, Kubernetes configuration should be maintained as version-controlled manifests or Helm-based configuration. The pipeline should validate YAML, check required fields, review policy requirements, and apply changes using an authorized deployment identity. Deployment automation should monitor rollout status and fail when the workload does not reach its expected availability state.\n\nKeep application build artifacts separate from environment-specific deployment configuration. Promote the same tested image digest between environments. Use controlled approvals for production changes and preserve deployment history so that engineers can identify which commit, image, and configuration produced the running workload.\n\n### 8. Production security and reliability practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC, dedicated service accounts, secure secret handling, resource requests and limits, appropriate health probes, and network restrictions where required. Avoid privileged containers and unnecessary host access. Plan for node failure, Pod rescheduling, application startup time, storage recovery, and external service dependencies.\n\nMonitor cluster and application health through metrics, logs, and events. Define actionable alerts for workload availability, resource pressure, failed scheduling, restart spikes, persistent storage errors, and network failures. Test recovery procedures in a non-production environment before depending on them during an incident.\n\n### 9. Practical engineer checklist\n\n- Confirm the correct Kubernetes cluster, AWS account, Region, and namespace.\n- Identify the resource and controller responsible for this behavior.\n- Review the current configuration and desired state.\n- Verify dependencies such as nodes, networking, storage, and permissions.\n- Inspect events, status conditions, and application logs.\n- Apply changes through a reviewed and repeatable workflow.\n- Confirm the resulting resource state and application behavior.\n- Record the evidence, root cause, and recovery procedure."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-22-section-13",
        "title": "Production Hands-on Lab — Deployment Strategies and Rollbacks",
        "content": "## Production Hands-on Lab — Deployment Strategies and Rollbacks\n\n### 1. Detailed technical explanation\n\nExplain the Kubernetes resource fields and configuration decisions that control this behavior. Describe how the API server validates the manifest, which controller or component acts on it, how to apply the configuration, and how to verify the resulting live resource.\n\nProduction Kubernetes operations require repeatable deployment procedures, capacity planning, access controls, health checks, observability, backup and recovery planning, and controlled change management. Workloads should define resource requests and limits, appropriate probes, replica requirements, disruption budgets, and secure configuration. Deployment validation should confirm both Kubernetes resource health and actual application behavior.\n\n### 2. Purpose and engineering relevance\n\nThe purpose of this subject is to help engineers understand how 'Production Hands-on Lab — Deployment Strategies and Rollbacks' affects the behavior of 'Production Hands-on Lab' in a Kubernetes or Amazon EKS environment. It is important to understand the desired state, the live state, and the Kubernetes component responsible for reconciling differences between them. Kubernetes resources are declarative: engineers describe the intended configuration, submit it through the API, and controllers work continuously to make the cluster match that configuration.\n\nA DevOps engineer should understand which resource owns the configuration, which controller processes it, which dependent resources are created, and how the result becomes visible to the application. This knowledge is necessary when designing deployments, reviewing manifests, troubleshooting incidents, and planning production changes.\n\n### 3. Internal workflow and component interaction\n\nProduction Kubernetes operations require repeatable deployment procedures, capacity planning, access controls, health checks, observability, backup and recovery planning, and controlled change management. Workloads should define resource requests and limits, appropriate probes, replica requirements, disruption budgets, and secure configuration. Deployment validation should confirm both Kubernetes resource health and actual application behavior.\n\nThe normal engineering workflow is to inspect the existing namespace and resources, review the relevant manifest or configuration, validate the intended changes, apply them through an approved deployment process, and inspect the resulting resource conditions. The API server is the entry point for Kubernetes API operations. Controllers reconcile desired state, the scheduler makes placement decisions for unscheduled Pods, and kubelet manages assigned workloads on worker nodes.\n\nThe exact components involved depend on the resource being configured. A Service relies on selectors and endpoints; a Deployment relies on ReplicaSets and Pods; a PersistentVolumeClaim relies on storage provisioning and binding; and an Ingress relies on an implementing controller. Understanding these dependencies prevents engineers from treating a symptom as the root cause.\n\n### 4. Practical commands and implementation\n\nThe following commands provide a starting point for inspecting the relevant Kubernetes or EKS environment. Replace placeholder values with the real namespace, resource name, cluster name, and AWS Region. Read command output before making changes.\n\nkubectl get deployments,pods,services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nkubectl rollout status deployment/DEPLOYMENT_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n\nUse the commands as diagnostic examples. For a configuration change, review the manifest, validate its syntax and resource fields, inspect the proposed change, and apply it through the repository's normal CI/CD process. Avoid applying an unreviewed manifest directly to a production cluster.\n\n### 5. Section-specific engineering guidance\n\nExplain the Kubernetes resource fields and configuration decisions that control this behavior. Describe how the API server validates the manifest, which controller or component acts on it, how to apply the configuration, and how to verify the resulting live resource.\n\nWhen documenting this subject, record the resource name, namespace, labels, selectors, relevant annotations, configuration values, dependencies, and expected status. Explain how the feature behaves during normal operation and what changes when a dependency becomes unhealthy. Include the operational effect on application availability, deployment speed, recovery time, and resource consumption.\n\n### 6. Verification and troubleshooting\n\nFor verification, inspect the live Kubernetes resource, its status conditions, related Pods, controller state, and recent events. Compare the observed state with the declared desired state. Confirm application behavior using readiness, logs, service connectivity, and relevant metrics rather than relying only on an API request returning successfully.\n\nProduction Kubernetes operations require repeatable deployment procedures, capacity planning, access controls, health checks, observability, backup and recovery planning, and controlled change management. Workloads should define resource requests and limits, appropriate probes, replica requirements, disruption budgets, and secure configuration. Deployment validation should confirm both Kubernetes resource health and actual application behavior.\n\nStart troubleshooting by capturing the current state before changing anything. Inspect resource status, events, controller logs, Pod descriptions, and application logs. Check whether the issue affects one Pod, one node, one namespace, or the entire cluster. Compare a failing resource with a healthy resource where possible. Identify the responsible component before selecting a corrective action.\n\nAfter remediation, verify that the resource reaches its expected status, dependent resources become healthy, application traffic succeeds, and no new warning events appear. Record the root cause and the corrective action so the same failure can be detected earlier in future deployments.\n\n### 7. CI/CD and DevOps integration\n\nIn a production CI/CD workflow, Kubernetes configuration should be maintained as version-controlled manifests or Helm-based configuration. The pipeline should validate YAML, check required fields, review policy requirements, and apply changes using an authorized deployment identity. Deployment automation should monitor rollout status and fail when the workload does not reach its expected availability state.\n\nKeep application build artifacts separate from environment-specific deployment configuration. Promote the same tested image digest between environments. Use controlled approvals for production changes and preserve deployment history so that engineers can identify which commit, image, and configuration produced the running workload.\n\n### 8. Production security and reliability practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC, dedicated service accounts, secure secret handling, resource requests and limits, appropriate health probes, and network restrictions where required. Avoid privileged containers and unnecessary host access. Plan for node failure, Pod rescheduling, application startup time, storage recovery, and external service dependencies.\n\nMonitor cluster and application health through metrics, logs, and events. Define actionable alerts for workload availability, resource pressure, failed scheduling, restart spikes, persistent storage errors, and network failures. Test recovery procedures in a non-production environment before depending on them during an incident.\n\n### 9. Practical engineer checklist\n\n- Confirm the correct Kubernetes cluster, AWS account, Region, and namespace.\n- Identify the resource and controller responsible for this behavior.\n- Review the current configuration and desired state.\n- Verify dependencies such as nodes, networking, storage, and permissions.\n- Inspect events, status conditions, and application logs.\n- Apply changes through a reviewed and repeatable workflow.\n- Confirm the resulting resource state and application behavior.\n- Record the evidence, root cause, and recovery procedure."
      },
      {
