        "content": "## Production Hands-on Lab — Troubleshooting and Diagnostics — Production Hands-on Lab [stage-10-topic-22-section-15]\n\n### 1. Detailed technical explanation\n\nExplain the observable symptom, likely causes, evidence to collect, diagnostic command sequence, and safe remediation options. Show how to narrow the issue to the API, controller, scheduler, node, network, storage, security, or application layer before changing configuration.\n\nProduction Kubernetes operations require repeatable deployment procedures, capacity planning, access controls, health checks, observability, backup and recovery planning, and controlled change management. Workloads should define resource requests and limits, appropriate probes, replica requirements, disruption budgets, and secure configuration. Deployment validation should confirm both Kubernetes resource health and actual application behavior.\n\n### 2. Purpose and engineering relevance\n\nThe purpose of this subject is to help engineers understand how 'Production Hands-on Lab — Troubleshooting and Diagnostics — Production Hands-on Lab [stage-10-topic-22-section-15]' affects the behavior of 'Production Hands-on Lab' in a Kubernetes or Amazon EKS environment. It is important to understand the desired state, the live state, and the Kubernetes component responsible for reconciling differences between them. Kubernetes resources are declarative: engineers describe the intended configuration, submit it through the API, and controllers work continuously to make the cluster match that configuration.\n\nA DevOps engineer should understand which resource owns the configuration, which controller processes it, which dependent resources are created, and how the result becomes visible to the application. This knowledge is necessary when designing deployments, reviewing manifests, troubleshooting incidents, and planning production changes.\n\n### 3. Internal workflow and component interaction\n\nProduction Kubernetes operation